candidates.problem:
This is false as stated. If $G$ is a filter meeting $A^{\downarrow}$, then $G\cap A$ has at most one element (since $A$ is an antichain and $G$ is a filter), but it need not have exactly one element. For example, let $\PP=\{0,1\}$ with $0<1$, $A=\{1\}$ (maximal antichain), and $G=\{0\}$... but $\{0\}$ is not a filter (not upward closed). Take $G=\{0,1\}$: this meets $A^{\downarrow}=\{0,1\}$ and $G\cap A=\{1\}$, one element. Counterexample: poset $\omega$, $A=\{5\}$ maximal antichain, $A^{\downarrow}=\{0,1,2,3,4,5\}$. Let $G=\{0,1,2,3\}$ — not a filter (not upward closed in $\omega$). A filter in $\omega$ must be upward closed, so any filter containing an element $\ge 5$ contains $5$. But a filter could be $\{n:n\ge 3\}$, which meets $A^{\downarrow}$ (contains $3,4,5$) and $G\cap A=\{5\}$. To get $G\cap A=\emptyset$ while $G\cap A^{\downarrow}
eq\emptyset$: take $G=\{n:n\ge 3\}$ and $A=\{5\}$ — then $G\cap A=\{5\}
eq\emptyset$. Try $A=\{5\}$, $G=\{n:n\ge 6\}$: $G\cap A^{\downarrow}=\emptyset$. So need $G$ meeting $A^{\downarrow}$ but not $A$: $G=\{n:n\ge 4\}$ meets $A^{\downarrow}$ (contains $4$) and $G\cap A=\{5\}$. Hmm. In a linear order, if $G$ meets $A^{\downarrow}$ then since $G$ is upward closed and $A^{\downarrow}$ is downward closed below $a$, meeting $A^{\downarrow}$ means some $q\le a$ is in $G$; upward closure gives $a\in G$. So in linear orders it works. But in general posets it fails: take $\PP=\{a,b,c\}$ with $c<a$, $c<b$, $a,b$ incomparable. $A=\{a,b\}$ is a maximal antichain. $A^{\downarrow}=\{a,b,c\}$. Let $G=\{c,a\}$ — is this a filter? Upward closed: $c<a$, $a\in G$, ok; $c<b$ but $b
otin G$, so not upward closed. Need $G$ upward closed: $G=\{a,c\}$ fails. $G=\{a\}$: upward closed (nothing above $a$), meets $A^{\downarrow}$, $G\cap A=\{a\}$, one element. $G=\{c\}$: not upward closed. So in this poset it holds. The general failure: $G$ meets $A^{\downarrow}$ via $q\le a$; $q\in G$ and $q\le a$ does not imply $a\in G$ unless $G$ is upward closed — but filters are upward closed, so $a\in G$. Hence $G\cap A
eq\emptyset$. And at most one by antichain. So (b) is actually correct. Therefore the error must be elsewhere.
